    {
      "short": "Add a cache",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "title": "Caching — and the stampede that comes with it",
      "body": [
        "Most read traffic was the <em>same data over and over</em> — perfect cache material. Redis serves hot reads from memory in ~1ms and absorbs them before they reach the database.",
        "Then a hot key expired and thousands of concurrent requests all missed at the same instant — a <span class=\"term\">cache stampede</span> that flattened the DB worse than having no cache at all, because you'd sized the DB assuming the cache."
      ],
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "A cache is borrowed capacity: always ask 'what happens on the miss path?' — because one day the cache will be cold."
      },
      "metrics": {
        "users": 60000,
        "rps": 1680,
        "p95": 60,
        "errors": 0.4
      },
